    #!/usr/bin/env bash
    # Milestone 3 part 2 P2 gate.log (specs/0024-rich-text.md, "P2: the
    # comment runs" and "Phases"): the spec pinned by the hash of its committed
    # blob, the full gate on a cold cache, the phase's commits by role and by the
    # paths they touch, each commit message's form and the count rule on every
    # commit, the doc commit's two files against the hashes planning's message
    # gave, each commit's diff hashed, each code commit held to its area and its
    # files' statuses, no styles file, no manifest and no lock change, the gate
    # files checked against the diff, the scripts parsed, the new and modified
    # sources scored, the public fn and const first lines listed without and
    # with the pair cfg of the features comments and richtext, the consumer
    # crate that names the two items the pair gates with comments alone and with
    # both, clippy and doc over the crate's feature sets and clippy over the
    # probe crate's, the crate's tests with the features off, comments alone and
    # comments with richtext on both targets, the base's tests edited only where
    # the comment entries gain their runs, shown line by line, the four DEBUG
    # binary hashes, this script's own stderr counted, and the working tree's
    # status with planning's uncommitted spec named.
    #
    # Usage: m3p2-p2-gate.sh <phase commit> > <file outside the tree>; the caller
    # moves the output to records/logs/m3p2-p2/gate.log. The log prints its own
    # invocation, and every environment input it reads on a labelled line after it.
    #
    # This is a FILE, not an inline block, so "$0" names it and the log prints the
    # script rather than the shell binary.
    #
    # Its own text is committed inside the log, so it holds no office file name, no
    # path shape and no address: the office regex is read from check.sh, the
    # pre-P6d path forms are read from git, and every control fixture is assembled
    # at run time from pieces (specs/0001-bootstrap.md, as in every earlier gate log).
